      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A concrete crack filler has one job, and it is not to make the crack disappear. It is to keep water out of the slab while the crack keeps doing whatever it was going to do. A crack that opens and closes with the seasons needs something that stretches, a crack that has stopped moving can take something rigid, and a hairline needs something thin enough to get inside it. Pick the filler for the crack, not the other way round.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked and Tested
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with the crack fillers that are actually stocked where homeowners shop, on Amazon and at the big-box stores, and narrowed them to products with published technical data. A filler that will not tell you its maximum joint depth, its movement capability or its cure time is asking you to guess, and guessing is how repairs end up peeling out of the crack by spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each product was sorted by the job it is built for rather than ranked on a single scale. A self-leveling polyurethane and a two-part epoxy are not really competitors. One is designed to flex with a moving joint and the other is designed to bond a crack that has stopped moving into a single rigid piece. Scoring them against each other would be like comparing a hinge with a weld.
          </p>

          <p>
            We filled a set of test cracks in an old garage apron and a patio slab: hairlines under a sixteenth of an inch, quarter-inch cracks, and a half-inch control joint that visibly opens in cold weather. We judged how easily each product went in, whether it self-leveled or needed tooling, how long it stayed tacky, and how the cured bead looked against weathered gray concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three things were weighted above everything else. Movement capability, because most outdoor slab cracks move and a rigid filler in a moving crack fails at the bond line. Adhesion to slightly dusty, imperfect concrete, because no homeowner prepares a crack like a lab technician. And honesty of the label, because a product that claims to fix every crack usually fixes none of them especially well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices were checked across retailers but are not quoted here, because caulk-tube pricing moves constantly. Where a product costs meaningfully more per linear foot than the alternatives, we say so in its review.
          </p>
