        <div id="crack-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hairline, Wide or Moving: Diagnose the Crack First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you buy anything, spend five minutes on the crack itself. Three questions decide the product: how wide is it, how deep is it, and is it still moving. Getting those answers right matters far more than which brand you pick.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hairline cracks, under 1/8 inch</h3>

          <p>
            Hairlines are usually shrinkage cracks from when the slab first cured, and most of them are cosmetic. The problem is that a thick caulk cannot get into them; it bridges the top, bonds to the dusty edges and peels off. A thin liquid filler that wicks into the crack is the right tool. Many hairlines do not need filling at all and are better protected by a penetrating sealer over the whole slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wide cracks, 1/8 to 1/2 inch and beyond</h3>

          <p>
            This is the range where caulk-tube fillers do their best work. Anything deeper than about half an inch needs backer rod first so the sealant ends up the right thickness. Anything wider than roughly an inch and a half has stopped being a crack repair and become a patching or slab-replacement job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moving cracks</h3>

          <p>
            The simplest test is to draw a pencil line across the crack in summer and measure it again in midwinter. If the gap has widened, or one side has lifted, the crack is live. Moving cracks need a flexible sealant rated for joint movement. Epoxy and cement patches crack out of them within a season, often pulling a sliver of concrete off the edge as they go.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more distinction matters. A crack where one side sits higher than the other is a displacement problem, not a gap problem. Filling it keeps water out, but it will not remove the trip hazard, so read our <a href="/driveway-crack-repair/">driveway crack repair guide</a> before you commit to a fix.
          </p>
        </div>

