        <div id="filler-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Polyurethane vs Epoxy vs Patching Compound
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every concrete crack filler on the shelf falls into one of five families, and each behaves in a way the packaging rarely spells out.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flexibility</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best Crack</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid On</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Self-leveling polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High, around 25% joint movement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Horizontal moving cracks and joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Walls and steep slopes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gun-grade polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Walls, steps, foundations</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hairlines it cannot enter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic latex liquid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low to moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hairlines and stable cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joints that open in winter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None, fully rigid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Static cracks and broken edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Any crack that still moves</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premixed or cement patch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chips, popouts, shallow holes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Live cracks and joints</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Polyurethane is the default for outdoor slabs for a simple reason: concrete moves. Slabs expand in summer heat, shrink in winter cold and shift with the soil beneath them, and a urethane bead stretches with that movement instead of tearing. The self-leveling versions pour flat and give a clean, level surface on driveways, while the gun-grade versions stay where you put them on a wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy is the opposite. A two-part epoxy cures harder than the concrete around it and bonds the two sides of a crack into one piece. That is exactly what you want in a crack that has stopped moving, such as an old shrinkage crack in a basement slab or a broken step nose. In a live crack, the concrete next to the epoxy becomes the weak point, and a new crack opens right beside the repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy injection, where resin is pumped into a crack through surface ports, is a structural repair for poured foundation walls and a different job from filling a slab crack. Homeowner injection kits exist, and for leaking basement walls many use polyurethane foam rather than epoxy. Our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">basement crack repair guide</a> covers when each approach is worth using.
          </p>

          <p>
            Patching compounds, whether acrylic premixes or vinyl-modified cement, are for filling volume: chipped edges, popouts and shallow holes. They are not crack sealants, and a patch smeared into a moving crack will reopen along the same line. For broad surface damage across a whole slab, a <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/">concrete resurfacer</a> is the better tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backer-rod" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Backer Rod and Joint Depth: The Step Everyone Skips
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you remember one technical point from this guide, make it this: a sealant bead has an ideal shape, and more is not better. Flexible sealants are designed to stretch in a joint that is roughly twice as wide as it is deep. A bead that is too deep cannot stretch evenly, and the stress concentrates at the bond line until one side tears loose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backer rod is a round, compressible foam cord that you press into the crack before sealing. It does three jobs. It sets the depth of the sealant so the bead has the right proportions. It stops the sealant from bonding to the bottom of the crack, so the bead only grips the two sides and can flex freely between them. And it saves product, sometimes a startling amount, because a deep crack can swallow a whole tube before you see any fill.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Width twice the depth, and never deeper than 1/2 inch.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the crack is deeper than about half an inch, press in backer rod or packed sand first. The sealant should finish between 1/4 and 1/2 inch deep, bonded to the two sides only.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The manufacturers are specific about this. Sika's self-leveling data calls for a sealant depth between 1/4 and 1/2 inch in moving joints, the full 1/2 inch in horizontal joints that take traffic, and a two-to-one width-to-depth ratio. Sashco tells you to use backer rod in any crack deeper than 1/2 inch and wider than 1/4 inch. Loctite does not recommend its polyurethane in joints deeper than 1/2 inch without backer rod.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy closed-cell rod for outdoor work, sized about a quarter wider than the crack so it stays put under compression. Push it in with a blunt tool such as a putty knife or a wooden paint stirrer, never a screwdriver tip. A punctured closed-cell rod can release gas and leave bubbles in the sealant above it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For narrow, deep cracks where rod will not fit, pack the bottom with clean, dry sand and seal over it. That is a workable substitute on horizontal slabs, and it is the same technique we describe for wide cracks in our <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/">asphalt crack filler guide</a>, where the principle is identical even though the product chemistry is not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="horizontal-vertical" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Horizontal vs Vertical Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            The orientation of the crack decides the consistency of the product, and this is where the self-leveling versus gun-grade split matters most.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a horizontal surface, self-leveling sealant is the better choice by a wide margin. It flows into the crack, finds its own level and cures with a flat, smooth top that sheds water and does not catch a snow shovel. You get a neater result without tooling, and a flat bead is less likely to be torn out by tires or foot traffic. On a sloped driveway, though, a thin self-leveler will run downhill. Lay down short sections and let them skin over, or switch to a non-sag product for the steepest stretch.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a vertical surface, a self-leveling product simply runs out of the crack and down the wall. You need a non-sag, gun-grade sealant that holds its bead where you put it. Foundation walls, retaining walls, step risers and the gap where a slab meets a house wall all fall into this group. A textured product that tools to a sandy finish is also far less visible on a wall than a glossy caulk line.
          </p>

          <p>
            The junction between a driveway or patio and the house foundation deserves special attention. It is usually an isolation joint designed to let the slab move independently of the house, and it is one of the most common places water finds its way into a basement. Seal it with a flexible polyurethane over backer rod, never with mortar or epoxy, which lock the slab to the house and crack at the first hard freeze.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cracks on the underside of a structural slab, such as a garage ceiling or a concrete porch roof, are a job for a non-sag product at most, and often for a professional. If a crack in a slab above your head is getting wider, it is not a filler question at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep-application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Prep and Fill a Concrete Crack
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most crack repairs fail at the bond, not in the product, and the bond is decided by preparation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by opening the crack up. Use a wire brush, a cold chisel or an angle grinder with a crack-chasing blade to remove loose concrete and give the crack clean, sound sides. A slightly widened crack with square walls takes sealant far better than a ragged hairline full of grit. Then blow it out with compressed air or a shop vacuum. A crack that looks clean usually still has a layer of dust on its walls, and sealant bonds to the dust instead of the concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture matters more than most labels admit. Polyurethane sealants cure with moisture from the air, but they still need a dry crack to bond; a damp crack produces bubbles and weak adhesion. Wait at least a day after rain or pressure washing, and fill on a dry day with the slab comfortably above 40 degrees. Avoid filling in blazing midday sun, when the crack is at its narrowest and the fresh sealant will be stretched hard as the slab cools overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Install backer rod if the crack needs it, cut the nozzle to match the crack width, and fill in one steady pass. For self-leveling products, stop slightly below the surface and let the product settle; overfilling leaves a raised strip that tires scuff away. For gun-grade products, tool the bead with a gloved finger or a plastic spoon within a few minutes, before it skins over.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep traffic off the repair until it has cured, which means a day or more for most sealants and longer in cool weather. A good <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">caulk gun</a> with a smooth, high thrust ratio makes the 29 ounce tubes far easier to control along a long joint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="structural" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When a Crack Is Not a Filler Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crack fillers seal. They do not hold concrete together, stop settlement or reverse frost heave, and some cracks are telling you something a tube of sealant cannot fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of cracks wider than about a quarter inch that are still growing, cracks where one side has dropped or lifted noticeably, and stair-step cracks running through block walls. In a foundation, a horizontal crack along a basement wall can signal soil pressure pushing the wall inward and deserves an inspection by a structural engineer rather than a bead of caulk. Vertical and diagonal hairline cracks in poured walls are far more common and usually benign.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a driveway, a web of interconnected cracks with sunken sections usually means the base under the slab has washed out or was never compacted properly. Filling each crack will keep water out for a while, and that is worth doing to slow the damage, but the slab will keep breaking up. At that stage the conversation is about replacement or slab lifting rather than which filler to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pattern of cracking can also point to drainage. Cracks that cluster where a downspout discharges, or along an edge where water pools after rain, are often a sign that the soil underneath is being saturated and softened. Redirecting that water will do more to stop new cracks than any filler on this list.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means filling is pointless on a failing slab. Keeping water out of the cracks slows freeze-thaw damage and buys time. Just budget for it as maintenance rather than a cure, and do not spend premium money sealing a slab you plan to replace within a couple of years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-filling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. After Filling: Sealing and Resurfacing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filling the cracks is usually the first step of a larger job, and the order you do things in matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to seal the whole slab, fill the cracks first and let the filler cure fully before sealing over it. Most sealers can go over cured polyurethane, but check the sealant's data sheet, since some need a longer cure before they will accept a coating. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealer</a> guide covers penetrating and film-forming sealers for patios and walks, and for driveways specifically see our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a>. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer is also the best defense against hairlines too fine to fill.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the surface is scaled, pitted or covered in many small cracks, a resurfacer may be a better investment than dozens of individual repairs. Fill the larger cracks and every control joint with flexible sealant, then resurface the rest of the slab. Remember that a resurfacer is rigid, so any live crack underneath will reflect straight through it; the joints have to be preserved, not buried. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/">best concrete resurfacer</a> walks through that process.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect to revisit filled cracks every few years. Polyurethane sealants in traffic areas last well when installed at the right depth, but UV, tire scuffing and extreme movement all wear them down. A repair that has pulled away on one side can usually be cut out and re-sealed without starting from scratch.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your driveway is asphalt rather than concrete, none of these products are the right choice. Asphalt needs a flexible rubberized or bitumen-based filler, which we compare in our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">driveway crack filler roundup</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using epoxy in a moving crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy cures harder than the concrete around it, so when a live crack opens in winter, the concrete beside the repair breaks instead of the epoxy. You end up with the original crack plus a new one next to it. Save rigid epoxy for cracks that have demonstrably stopped moving, and use flexible polyurethane everywhere else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a deep crack without backer rod</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bead that is too deep cannot stretch evenly and tears at the bond line. It also wastes product, sometimes a whole tube every few feet. Any crack deeper than about half an inch needs backer rod or packed sand first, so the sealant finishes at the depth the manufacturer actually designed it for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over dust and damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealant bonds to whatever is on the crack walls. If that is loose grit or a film of dust, the bead peels out with it within months, and a damp crack leaves bubbles and weak adhesion. Wire brush, vacuum or blow the crack clean, and wait for a dry day after rain or washing before filling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using self-leveling sealant on a wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Self-leveling products are thin by design and will run straight out of a vertical crack and down the face of a foundation or step riser. On walls and steep slopes you need a non-sag, gun-grade polyurethane that holds its bead. Check the label for horizontal-only wording before you buy anything for a wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Burying control joints under patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Control joints exist so the slab cracks where it is supposed to. Fill them with rigid patching compound or skim them over with resurfacer and the slab simply cracks somewhere else, usually right beside the joint. Keep every joint open and seal it with flexible polyurethane over backer rod instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best concrete crack filler for a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most driveway cracks between 1/8 and 1/2 inch, a self-leveling polyurethane such as Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant or Quikrete Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant is the right choice. It flexes with seasonal movement, levels flat so tires and shovels do not catch it, and holds up to traffic when installed at the proper depth over backer rod. Hairlines need a thinner liquid filler.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular caulk to fill cracks in concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is rated for concrete. Standard household acrylic or silicone caulk is not designed for the movement, abrasion and UV exposure of an outdoor slab, and most will fail within a season. Look for a polyurethane or hybrid polymer labeled for concrete, and check whether it is self-leveling for horizontal cracks or non-sag for vertical ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide a crack can concrete crack filler fix?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most caulk-tube sealants handle cracks from about 1/8 inch up to 1/2 inch comfortably, and self-leveling joint sealants can fill joints over an inch wide when set on backer rod. Past about an inch and a half you are no longer sealing a crack; the edges usually need patching with a cement-based repair product, or the section needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I fill hairline cracks in concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Many hairline shrinkage cracks are cosmetic and stable. Where water can get in and freeze, a thin liquid filler that wicks into the crack helps, and a penetrating sealer over the whole slab protects against water entering through hairlines too fine to fill. Thick caulk over a hairline just bridges the surface and peels off within months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete crack filler last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly installed polyurethane sealant in a clean, dry crack at the correct depth typically lasts several years, and longer out of direct traffic and sun. Acrylic liquids and patching compounds wear out sooner, especially in freeze-thaw climates. Inspect filled cracks each fall and re-seal any section that has pulled away from one side before winter sets in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is epoxy or polyurethane better for concrete cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on whether the crack moves. Polyurethane stays flexible and is right for outdoor slab cracks and joints that open and close with temperature. Epoxy is rigid and structural, so it suits cracks that have stopped moving and repairs where you want the two sides bonded together, such as a broken step edge or a stable basement slab crack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal or paint over concrete crack filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, once it has cured. Most polyurethane and acrylic crack fillers accept a concrete sealer or paint after the cure time on the label, often a few days. Check the data sheet, since some sealants need longer before coating. Always fill cracks before sealing the slab, never after, so the sealer does not interfere with the filler's bond.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

