        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best concrete crack filler is the one matched to the crack. For most horizontal cracks in driveways, patios and garage floors, that is a self-leveling polyurethane like Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant, installed over backer rod at the right depth. For walls and steps, use a non-sag polyurethane like Loctite PL; for hairlines, a thin liquid like Quikrete Concrete Crack Seal; and save epoxy for cracks that have genuinely stopped moving.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, the repair lives or dies on prep. Clean the crack back to sound concrete, fill it dry, respect the depth limit on the label, and check it again each fall. Do that and an ordinary tube of sealant will do more for your slab than any miracle product on the shelf.
